  q(HCE,'Which program provides health coverage for active-duty military members, retirees and their families?','TRICARE',['CHIP','Medicaid','Workers’ compensation'],'B','TRICARE is the Department of Defense health program; veterans may also use VA health care.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A health care organization appoints someone to oversee its program to prevent fraud and follow laws. What is this role?','Compliance officer',['Medical examiner','Executor','Guardian ad litem'],'C','Compliance programs include written policies, training, audits and a compliance officer.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Another patient overhears part of a conversation despite reasonable privacy safeguards. Under HIPAA, what is this?','An incidental disclosure that is generally permitted',['A criminal breach that must be reported to police','A violation that ends the employee’s license','A required disclosure for public health'],'D','HIPAA allows incidental disclosures if reasonable safeguards and minimum necessary rules are followed.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A patient pays in full out of pocket and asks that the visit not be shared with their health plan. What must the provider do?','Honor the request',['Refuse, because plans must receive all information','Charge the patient a penalty fee','Report the patient to the insurer'],'A','HIPAA requires providers to honor this restriction when the patient pays the full cost themselves.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Under HIPAA, a patient may ask for a list of certain disclosures of their health information. What is this called?','An accounting of disclosures',['A Notice of Privacy Practices','A business associate agreement','A subpoena duces tecum'],'B','Patients can request an accounting of disclosures made for purposes other than treatment, payment and operations.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'An employee files an OSHA complaint about unsafe conditions. What protection does the law provide?','Protection from retaliation by the employer',['Automatic promotion to a supervisor role','Immunity from all job performance reviews','A guaranteed pay raise'],'C','OSHA forbids employers from punishing workers who report hazards.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A hospital decides which procedures a physician may perform there. What is this called?','Granting clinical privileges',['Granting a medical license','Issuing a DEA number','Certifying a specialty board'],'D','After credentialing, hospitals grant privileges that define what a provider may do in that facility.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Why is a chain-of-custody form used for an employee drug test?','To document everyone who handled the specimen so it can be used as evidence',['To bill the employer for the cost of the test and any follow-up testing needed','To show the patient’s insurance coverage','To schedule the employee’s next appointment'],'A','An unbroken chain of custody shows the sample was not tampered with.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'In most states, when can a person be held for involuntary psychiatric evaluation?','When they are a danger to themselves or others due to mental illness',['Whenever any family member or friend requests it in writing for any reason','When they refuse to take any medication','When they miss two appointments'],'B','Involuntary holds are limited in time and require due process because they restrict liberty.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'An employee posts a photo of an unusual X-ray online with no name but with details that identify the patient. What is this?','A HIPAA violation, because the patient can still be identified',['Allowed, because no name was included','Allowed, because X-rays are public information','Required for medical education'],'C','Any information that could reasonably identify a patient is protected, even without a name.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A police officer asks for a patient’s records about a minor fender-bender. What is usually needed before releasing them?','A court order, warrant, subpoena or the patient’s authorization',['Only the officer’s verbal request','Only the officer’s badge number','Nothing, because police can access any record'],'D','HIPAA allows disclosure to law enforcement only in specific situations, such as a court order.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A Medicare patient may have to pay for a test that Medicare may deny. Which form should the patient sign beforehand?','An Advance Beneficiary Notice (ABN)',['A Notice of Privacy Practices','A living will','An incident report'],'A','The ABN lets the patient decide whether to receive the service knowing they may be billed.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A patient must pay the first $1,000 of covered costs each year before the plan pays. What is this amount?','The deductible',['The copayment','The premium','The capitation rate'],'B','After the deductible is met, the plan begins sharing costs, often through coinsurance.',FREM),
  q(BIO,'In which process are eggs fertilized by sperm in a laboratory before an embryo is placed in the uterus?','In vitro fertilization (IVF)',['Artificial insemination','Reproductive cloning','Gene therapy'],'C','IVF raises ethical issues such as the fate of extra embryos and multiple pregnancies.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which 1973 Supreme Court case first recognized a constitutional right to abortion before it was overturned in 2022?','Roe v. Wade',['In re Quinlan','Buck v. Bell','Cruzan v. Missouri'],'D','Dobbs v. Jackson (2022) overturned Roe and returned abortion regulation to the states.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which gene-editing tool can make precise changes to DNA and raises questions about editing human embryos?','CRISPR',['MRI','PCR testing','Dialysis'],'A','CRISPR-Cas9 allows targeted DNA editing; germline editing is widely considered unethical for now.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'A couple is tested to see whether each has one copy of a gene for cystic fibrosis. What is this testing called?','Carrier screening',['Newborn screening','Predictive testing for adult-onset disease','Paternity testing'],'B','Carriers usually have no symptoms but can pass the gene to children.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Parents sue because they were not told about a test that would have detected a serious condition before birth. What is this called?','A wrongful birth claim',['A wrongful death claim','A breach of privacy claim','A loss of consortium claim'],'C','Wrongful birth claims are brought by parents; wrongful life claims are brought on behalf of the child.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'When an incapacitated patient’s wishes are unknown, which standard should a surrogate use?','The best interest standard',['Substituted judgment based on known wishes','The reasonable physician standard','The highest bidder standard'],'D','The surrogate weighs benefits and burdens to decide what would most help the patient.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'In research with children, what is the child’s own agreement to take part called?','Assent',['Consent','Waiver','Endorsement'],'A','Parents give permission (consent), and children old enough to understand are asked for their assent.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'A dying patient with unbearable symptoms is given medication to lower consciousness until death. What is this called?','Palliative sedation',['Active euthanasia','Reproductive cloning','Passive immunity'],'B','Palliative sedation aims to relieve suffering, not to cause death, and is supported by the principle of double effect.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which factor is NOT used to decide who receives a donor organ in the United States?','The patient’s wealth or social status',['Medical urgency of the patient’s condition','Blood type and tissue match with the donor','Time spent on the national waiting list'],'C','Organ allocation is based on medical criteria, compatibility, waiting time and geography, not social worth.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Parents refuse a lifesaving blood transfusion for their child for religious reasons. What may the hospital do?','Seek a court order to give the transfusion',['Respect the refusal with no further action','Wait until the child turns 18','Ask the child to sign a consent form'],'D','Courts often order lifesaving treatment for minors, even when parents object.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Why do research rules give prisoners extra protection?','Their situation may limit their ability to freely say no',['They are always paid more than other subjects','They cannot sign any legal documents','They are immune to research risks'],'A','Prisoners, children and other vulnerable groups get added safeguards against coercion.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'A 14-year-old’s parents want her tested for the Huntington disease gene, which causes symptoms in adulthood. What do most guidelines advise?','Wait until she is an adult and can decide for herself',['Test her immediately without her knowledge','Test her only if she has good grades','Test her and keep the result from everyone'],'B','Predictive testing for adult-onset disease is usually deferred to protect the child’s future autonomy.',TAM)
